    {
      "id": "2y7i05r",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "1. Volcanoes"
    },
    {
      "id": "xldofb8",
      "type": "image",
      "imageId": "bs0adqt",
      "src": "/books/Guide/Cluster_013_GS & GK GUIDE BOOK/images/image_bs0adqt.jpeg"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "Volcanoes are classified by eruption frequency into Active, Dormant, and Extinct types, and by structure into Shield, Composite, and Dome volcanoes.",
        "The point of origin of an earthquake inside the Earth is called the Focus (Hypocenter), while the point on the surface vertically above it is the Epicenter.",
        "Primary (P) waves are compressional waves that travel fastest through solids, liquids, and gases.",
        "Secondary (S) waves are transverse waves that can only travel through solid media, proving the outer core is liquid.",
        "Earthquake magnitude measures energy released (Richter Scale), whereas earthquake intensity measures visible damage (Mercalli Scale)."
      ],
      "id": "t5p0a1u"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "z7nma0g",
      "content": "A volcano is an opening in the Earth's crust through which molten rock (magma), gases, and ash are expelled to the surface. When magma reaches the Earth's surface, it is called lava."
    },
    {
      "id": "0gb98aj",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "Structure of a Volcano"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "g14eaw4",
      "content": "• Crater – bowl-shaped opening at the top of a volcano."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "16yz3wq",
      "content": "• Vent – the channel through which magma reaches the surface."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "nqknpv1",
      "content": "• Magma Chamber – underground reservoir of molten rock."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "cv4okex",
      "content": "• Lava Flow – molten rock flowing out during eruption."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "1zkb7ed",
      "content": "• Ash and Gas – volcanic eruptions release ash, steam, and gases."
    },
    {
